@page "/actions"
@using System.Text.Json.Nodes
@inherits BlexComponentBase
@inject CounterStore Counter
@inject AccountStore Account
@inject WalletStore Wallet
@inject LedgerStore Ledger
@inject ProgressStore Progress

<DocPage Href="actions" Title="Actions"
         Lead="An action is a named, batched, recorded unit of change. Everything that mutates state becomes one - even a direct property assignment.">

    <div class="bb-card">
        <h2 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-primary"></span> The convention</h2>
        <p>
            Mark a method named <code>OnXxx</code> with <code>[BlexAction]</code>. The generator emits a
            public <code>Xxx(...)</code> wrapper that runs your method inside a dispatch: all the
            mutations it makes are folded into one notification and one time-travel entry.
        </p>

        <CodeBlock Code="@Basic" />
        <CodeBlock Code="@BasicGenerated" Title="Generated" />
    </div>

    <div class="bb-card">
        <h2 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-success"></span> Naming</h2>
        <p>
            The wrapper name comes from the method name with the <code>On</code> prefix stripped, or from
            <code>[BlexAction(Name = "…")]</code>. The <code>Name</code> is used in two different ways
            depending on whether it is a valid C# identifier:
        </p>

        <div class="bb-scroll">
            <table class="bb-table">
                <thead><tr><th>Declaration</th><th>Method you call</th><th>Label in DevTools / history</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td><code>[BlexAction] void OnIncrement()</code></td><td><code>Increment()</code></td><td><code>Increment</code></td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>[BlexAction(Name = "Bump")] void OnIncrement()</code></td><td><code>Bump()</code></td><td><code>Bump</code></td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>[BlexAction(Name = "Apply preset")] void OnApplyPreset()</code></td><td><code>ApplyPreset()</code></td><td><code>Apply preset</code></td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>
        <p>
            In other words: a name containing spaces (or anything else illegal in an identifier, or a C#
            keyword) is treated purely as a display label, and the method name still comes from the
            <code>On</code> prefix. A method that neither starts with <code>On</code> nor supplies a
            usable name is <code>BLEX002</code>.
        </p>
    </div>

    <div class="bb-card">
        <h2 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-warning"></span> Arguments become the payload</h2>
        <p>
            Action parameters are captured as name/value pairs and travel with the action to middleware,
            subscribers, the test harness and the DevTools monitor. Nothing to declare.
        </p>

        <CodeBlock Code="@Args" />

        <p>
            Capture is skipped entirely when nothing is observing - the generated wrapper checks
            <code>IsObserved</code> first, so an app with no middleware, no DevTools and no subscribers
            pays nothing for the payload.
        </p>

        <p>Parameter rules:</p>
        <ul class="bb-detail-list">
            <li>Default values and <code>params</code> are preserved on the wrapper.</li>
            <li><code>ref</code>, <code>out</code> and <code>in</code> parameters are rejected - the wrapper has to capture them by value (<code>BLEX013</code>).</li>
            <li>Generic action methods are rejected; the wrapper cannot carry type parameters (<code>BLEX014</code>).</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="bb-card" id="typed-actions">
        <h2 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-success"></span> Typed actions</h2>
        <p>
            A method call is only half of what an action is. Middleware, subscribers, history filters and
            tests also need to <em>recognize</em> an action after the fact and read its payload - and a
            string like <code>"Apply preset"</code> plus an <code>object</code> looked up by parameter name
            breaks silently the day someone renames either. So the generator also emits a descriptor for
            every action and effect, in a nested <code>Actions</code> class on the store.
        </p>

        <CodeBlock Code="@Typed" />
        <CodeBlock Code="@TypedGenerated" Title="Generated" />

        <p>The payload type follows the parameters:</p>
        <div class="bb-scroll">
            <table class="bb-table">
                <thead><tr><th>Declaration</th><th>Descriptor</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td><code>void OnIncrement()</code></td><td><code>BlexAction&lt;CounterStore&gt;</code></td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>void OnSetStep(int step)</code></td><td><code>BlexAction&lt;CounterStore, int&gt;</code> - the argument itself</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>void OnApplyPreset(int count, int step)</code></td><td><code>BlexAction&lt;CounterStore, (int Count, int Step)&gt;</code> - a tuple named after the parameters</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>Task OnSearch(string query, CancellationToken ct)</code></td><td><code>BlexAction&lt;SearchStore, string&gt;</code> - an effect's token is supplied by the wrapper, so it is not part of the payload</td></tr>
                    <tr><td><code>void OnTag(params string[] tags)</code></td><td><code>BlexAction&lt;S, string[]&gt;</code></td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>
        <p>
            When a parameter cannot name a tuple element - <code>rest</code>, <code>toString</code>, an
            <code>item2</code> in first position, two parameters that differ only in case - the tuple is
            simply left unnamed (<code>(string, int)</code>, read as <code>Item1</code>,
            <code>Item2</code>).
        </p>

        <CodeBlock Code="@TypedUse" Title="Matching, reading and dispatching" />

        <ApiTable Title="BlexAction&lt;TStore, TPayload&gt;" Rows="@DescriptorRows" />

        <p>
            The descriptor also plugs straight into the pipeline APIs:
            <a href="cross-store">manager.SubscribeToAction(CounterStore.Actions.ApplyPreset, ...)</a>,
            <a href="middleware">options.UseFilter(CounterStore.Actions.SetStep, ...)</a>, and the test
            harness's <a href="testing"><code>Log.PayloadsOf(...)</code></a>.
        </p>

        <Callout Kind="Callout.CalloutKind.Note" Title="When the name Actions is taken">
            <p>
                A store that already declares something called <code>Actions</code> - a property, a nested
                type, or state whose generated property is <code>Actions</code> - keeps compiling exactly
                as before, but without the typed descriptors, and the generator says so with warning
                <code>BLEX020</code>. Its actions stay dispatchable by name. Rename the member to get them.
            </p>
        </Callout>
    </div>

    <div class="bb-card" id="dispatch-by-name">
        <h2 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-warning"></span> Dispatching by name</h2>
        <p>
            Because every action is described as data, it can also be <em>sent</em> as data:
            <code>manager.DispatchAsync("store/Action", payload)</code> finds the action by its qualified
            name - the label DevTools shows - converts a JSON payload keyed by parameter name, and calls the
            generated wrapper. The action passes the filters, reaches middleware and is recorded exactly as
            a direct call would be. Direct assignments (<code>"counter/Set Count"</code>) and
            <code>"counter/ResetState"</code> are dispatchable too, and an optional parameter the payload
            leaves out takes its declared default.
        </p>
        <CodeBlock Code="@ByName" />
        <p>
            It is what the <a href="devtools">DevTools dispatcher</a> and the testing package's
            <a href="testing">action replay</a> are built on: a log recorded in one session dispatches
            again in another, which is the practical meaning of "a predictable state model". That makes the
            name an action's identity, so two actions dispatched under one name - two display labels alike,
            or a label such as <code>"Set Count"</code> that a direct assignment already records - fail the
            build with <a href="diagnostics#blex021"><code>BLEX021</code></a>.
        </p>

        <DemoCard Title="Actions as data"
                  Description="Pick a preset or write your own: the qualified name and the JSON payload are all DispatchAsync needs. The typed subscription below reads Apply preset's payload as (int Count, int Step)."
                  Source="@ByNameSource">
            <div class="bb-toolbar">
                @foreach (var preset in DispatchPresets)
                {
                    <button class="btn btn-secondary" @onclick="() => Preset(preset)">@preset.Label</button>
                }
            </div>
            <div class="bb-toolbar">
                @* Enter in either box dispatches. A key handler rather than a <form>: a form renders its action
                   from the NavigationManager, which the MCP server's renderer of this page does not have. *@
                <input type="text" aria-label="Qualified action name" value="@_dispatchName" @oninput="e => _dispatchName = e.Value?.ToString() ?? string.Empty" @onkeydown="OnDispatchKeyDown" class="demo-input-name" />
                <input type="text" aria-label="JSON payload" value="@_dispatchPayload" @oninput="e => _dispatchPayload = e.Value?.ToString() ?? string.Empty" @onkeydown="OnDispatchKeyDown" class="demo-input-grow" />
                <button class="btn btn-primary" @onclick="DispatchByName">DispatchAsync</button>
            </div>

            @if (_dispatchError is not null)
            {
                <div class="bb-note bb-note-danger" role="alert">
                    <strong class="demo-note-title">Not dispatched</strong>
                    <p>@_dispatchError</p>
                </div>
            }

            <div class="bb-stats">
                <div class="bb-stat">
                    <span class="bb-stat-label">Count</span>
                    <span class="bb-stat-value demo-accent"><Persisted>@Counter.Count</Persisted></span>
                </div>
                <div class="bb-stat">
                    <span class="bb-stat-label">Step</span>
                    <span class="bb-stat-value"><Persisted>@Counter.Step</Persisted></span>
                </div>
                <div class="bb-stat">
                    <span class="bb-stat-label">Last Apply preset payload</span>
                    <span class="bb-stat-value">@_lastPreset</span>
                </div>
            </div>
            <p class="bb-card-desc">
                Dispatchable on <code>counter</code>:
                @string.Join(", ", Counter.ActionDescriptors.Select(a => a.Name))
            </p>
        </DemoCard>
    </div>

    <div class="bb-card">
        <h2 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-primary"></span> Batching</h2>
        <p>
            This is the point of actions. Assigning three fields directly produces three actions and
            three renders; doing it inside one action produces one of each.
        </p>

        <DemoCard Title="Three ways to set the same two fields"
                  Description="Watch the notification counter - and the Inspector's action feed - after each button."
                  Source="@BatchSource">
            <div class="bb-stats">
                <div class="bb-stat">
                    <span class="bb-stat-label">Count</span>
                    <span class="bb-stat-value demo-accent"><Persisted>@Counter.Count</Persisted></span>
                </div>
                <div class="bb-stat">
                    <span class="bb-stat-label">Step</span>
                    <span class="bb-stat-value"><Persisted>@Counter.Step</Persisted></span>
                </div>
                <div class="bb-stat">
                    <span class="bb-stat-label">Notifications from last click</span>
                    <span class="bb-stat-value">@_lastBatchNotifications</span>
                </div>
                <div class="bb-stat">
                    <span class="bb-stat-label">Actions from last click</span>
                    <span class="bb-stat-value">@_lastBatchActions</span>
                </div>
            </div>
            <div class="bb-toolbar">
                <button class="btn demo-btn-danger" @onclick="DirectAssignment">Two direct assignments</button>
                <button class="btn btn-primary" @onclick="ViaAction">One [BlexAction] method</button>
                <button class="btn btn-primary" @onclick="ViaBatch">One Batch(…) call</button>
                <button class="btn btn-secondary" @onclick="() => Measure(Counter.ResetState)">ResetState()</button>
            </div>
            <p class="bb-card-desc">
                The first button dispatches <code>Set Count</code> and <code>Set Step</code> separately.
                The other two dispatch exactly one action and render once. Each click moves both fields on from
                where they are: assigning a field the value it already holds is no change, and notifies nobody.
            </p>
        </DemoCard>
    </div>

    <div class="bb-card">
        <h2 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-success"></span> Direct assignment: the "Set X" action</h2>
        <p>
            Generated properties have public setters (unless the store
            <a href="actions#enforce-actions">enforces actions</a>), and assigning one from outside any
            action is still a first-class action named <code>Set X</code>, carrying the assigned value as
            its payload. It is vetoable by middleware, recorded for undo/redo and visible in DevTools - so
            nothing can change state behind the pipeline's back.
        </p>
        <CodeBlock Code="@SetX" />
    </div>

    <div class="bb-card" id="enforce-actions">
        <h2 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-primary"></span> Enforcing actions</h2>
        <p>
            A <code>Set X</code> is still an anonymous change: it says <em>what</em> moved, never
            <em>why</em>, and any component holding the store can make one. A Flux store is supposed to
            guarantee the opposite - every change is a deliberate, named action. Opt in with
            <code>EnforceActions</code> and the guarantee is checked by the compiler:
        </p>
        <CodeBlock Code="@Enforce" />
        <ul class="bb-detail-list">
            <li>The generated state setters are <code>private</code>, so assigning one from outside the store does not compile (<code>CS0272</code>).</li>
            <li>An assignment the store's <em>own</em> code makes outside any action throws <code>InvalidOperationException</code> once the store is registered. Assignments in the constructor, before anyone can observe the store, are fine.</li>
            <li>Restores, hydration and <code>ResetState</code> are not assignments and work as usual; so do effects, whose bodies run inside their action.</li>
            <li>No <code>Set X</code> descriptors are offered for dispatch by name - such an action can never have been recorded.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
            Two-way binding needs a setter, so bind such a store's state through an action instead:
        </p>
        <CodeBlock Code="@EnforceBind" Language="razor" />

        <DemoCard Title="An enforcing store at runtime"
                  Description="This site's AccountStore is [BlexStore(EnforceActions = true)]. Deposit and Withdraw are actions and work. SneakBonus() is the store's own code assigning Balance outside any action - it compiles, and throws. The compile-time half cannot run in a browser: account.Balance = 0 from this page would not build."
                  Source="@EnforceSource">
            <div class="bb-toolbar">
                <button class="btn btn-primary" @onclick="() => RunEnforced(() => Account.Deposit(10))">Deposit(10)</button>
                <button class="btn btn-secondary" @onclick="() => RunEnforced(() => Account.Withdraw(10))">Withdraw(10)</button>
                <button class="btn demo-btn-danger" @onclick="() => RunEnforced(Account.SneakBonus)">SneakBonus() - outside an action</button>
                <button class="btn btn-secondary" @onclick="() => RunEnforced(Account.ResetState)">ResetState()</button>
            </div>

            @if (_enforceError is not null)
            {
                <div class="bb-note bb-note-danger" role="alert">
                    <strong class="demo-note-title">InvalidOperationException</strong>
                    <p>@_enforceError</p>
                </div>
            }

            <div class="bb-stats">
                <div class="bb-stat">
                    <span class="bb-stat-label">account/Balance</span>
                    <span class="bb-stat-value demo-accent">@Account.Balance.ToString("0.00")</span>
                </div>
            </div>
            <p class="bb-card-desc">
                Dispatchable on <code>account</code>: @string.Join(", ", Account.ActionDescriptors.Select(a => a.Name))
                - and no <code>Set Balance</code>, since nothing can ever have recorded one.
            </p>
        </DemoCard>
    </div>

    <div class="bb-card">
        <h2 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-warning"></span> Batch: ad-hoc grouping</h2>
        <p>
            When the grouping is a caller's concern rather than the store's, <code>Batch</code> wraps any
            set of mutations into one named action - Pinia's <code>$patch</code> or MobX's
            <code>runInAction</code>.
        </p>
        <CodeBlock Code="@BatchApi" />
    </div>

    <div class="bb-card">
        <h2 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-primary"></span> ResetState</h2>
        <p>
            Every store snapshots itself the first time it is registered with the manager.
            <code>ResetState()</code> restores that snapshot as a normal <code>ResetState</code> action:
            vetoable, observable, persisted and undoable. It is a no-op on a store that was never
            registered, and equally a no-op on a store that is <em>already</em> holding its initial state -
            resetting an untouched form notifies nobody, writes nothing and adds no undo step, the same
            rule every other mutation in Blex follows.
        </p>
        <CodeBlock Code="@Reset" />

        <p>
            <code>BlexManager.ResetAll()</code> does the same across every registered store - the usual
            sign-out move. Each store resets as its own action, so middleware can exempt individual
            stores and DevTools shows one entry per store rather than one opaque global wipe.
        </p>
        <CodeBlock Code="@ResetAll" Title="Application-wide reset" />
    </div>

    <div class="bb-card" id="rollback">
        <h2 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-danger"></span> When an action throws</h2>
        <p>
            An action is a C# method, and by default it behaves like one: what it changed before the throw
            stays changed - and, since it did change state, it is notified, recorded and persisted like any
            other action. The exception still reaches the caller and the action-error hooks.
        </p>
        <p>
            <code>[BlexStore(RollbackOnError = true)]</code> makes every synchronous action a transaction
            instead: a throw puts the store's fields back to what they held when the action started, and
            nothing is notified, recorded, persisted or added to the undo history. Each action level is its
            own transaction, so a nested action whose failure the caller catches is undone while the
            caller's own changes stay.
        </p>
        <CodeBlock Code="@Rollback" />

        <DemoCard Title="The same throw, with and without RollbackOnError"
                  Description="Two stores, one Withdraw each: take the money out, write the entry, then check the balance. Both start at 100.00. Withdraw 150 from each and compare what the throw leaves behind - and what it recorded."
                  Source="@RollbackDemoSource">
            <div class="bb-grid">
                <div class="bb-card">
                    <p class="bb-card-title">wallet <span class="bb-badge">default</span></p>
                    <div class="bb-stats">
                        <div class="bb-stat">
                            <span class="bb-stat-label">Balance</span>
                            <span class="bb-stat-value demo-accent">@Wallet.Balance.ToString("0.00")</span>
                        </div>
                        <div class="bb-stat">
                            <span class="bb-stat-label">Entries</span>
                            <span class="bb-stat-value">@Wallet.Entries.Count</span>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="bb-toolbar">
                        <button class="btn btn-secondary" @onclick='() => _rollbackNote = Attempt("wallet", () => Wallet.Withdraw(30))'>Withdraw(30)</button>
                        <button class="btn demo-btn-danger" @onclick='() => _rollbackNote = Attempt("wallet", () => Wallet.Withdraw(150))'>Withdraw(150) - throws</button>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <div class="bb-card">
                    <p class="bb-card-title">ledger <span class="bb-badge bb-badge-success">RollbackOnError</span></p>
                    <div class="bb-stats">
                        <div class="bb-stat">
                            <span class="bb-stat-label">Balance</span>
                            <span class="bb-stat-value demo-accent">@Ledger.Balance.ToString("0.00")</span>
                        </div>
                        <div class="bb-stat">
                            <span class="bb-stat-label">Entries</span>
                            <span class="bb-stat-value">@Ledger.Entries.Count</span>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="bb-toolbar">
                        <button class="btn btn-secondary" @onclick='() => _rollbackNote = Attempt("ledger", () => Ledger.Withdraw(30))'>Withdraw(30)</button>
                        <button class="btn demo-btn-danger" @onclick='() => _rollbackNote = Attempt("ledger", () => Ledger.Withdraw(150))'>Withdraw(150) - throws</button>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>
            <div class="bb-toolbar">
                <button class="btn btn-secondary" @onclick="ResetLedgers">ResetState() both</button>
            </div>
            @if (_rollbackNote.Length > 0)
            {
                <p class="bb-card-desc" role="status">@_rollbackNote</p>
            }
        </DemoCard>

        <Callout Kind="Callout.CalloutKind.Note" Title="What a rollback covers">
            <p>
                The snapshot is a shallow copy of the <code>[BlexState]</code> fields - one small array per
                action, no serialization - so it undoes assignments, which is how a Blex store changes, not
                mutations inside an object a field points to. It covers this store only, and synchronous
                actions only: an async action has already shown the UI what it changed before its first
                <code>await</code>.
            </p>
        </Callout>

        <h3 id="transactions">Across stores</h3>
        <p>
            <code>manager.Transaction(...)</code> is the same promise for a unit of work that spans stores: if the
            body throws, every store it changed is put back the way it was when the transaction began, and the
            exception reaches the caller. The actions inside run and record as usual; the putting back is one
            restore, like an undo - subscriptions re-evaluate once, persistence writes the restored state back, and
            the undo steps those actions recorded no longer hold what was taken back. Stores the body left alone are
            not touched. Every store is serialized when it begins, so keep it for multi-store operations; like
            <code>RollbackOnError</code>, it is synchronous only.
        </p>
        <CodeBlock Code="@TransactionSample" />

        <DemoCard Title="One unit of work across two stores"
                  Description="Each button runs one transfer inside manager.Transaction: Withdraw from the wallet, Deposit into the ledger. Whatever throws in the body - the bank declining afterwards, or the wallet's own Withdraw - puts both stores back where the transaction found them, though the wallet has no RollbackOnError of its own."
                  Source="@TransactionDemoSource">
            <div class="bb-toolbar">
                <button class="btn btn-primary" @onclick="() => Transfer(20, decline: false)">Transfer 20</button>
                <button class="btn demo-btn-danger" @onclick="() => Transfer(20, decline: true)">Transfer 20, then the bank declines</button>
                <button class="btn demo-btn-danger" @onclick="() => Transfer(500, decline: false)">Transfer 500 - more than the wallet holds</button>
                <button class="btn btn-secondary" @onclick="ResetLedgers">ResetState() both</button>
            </div>
            <div class="bb-stats">
                <div class="bb-stat">
                    <span class="bb-stat-label">wallet/Balance</span>
                    <span class="bb-stat-value demo-accent">@Wallet.Balance.ToString("0.00")</span>
                </div>
                <div class="bb-stat">
                    <span class="bb-stat-label">ledger/Balance</span>
                    <span class="bb-stat-value demo-accent">@Ledger.Balance.ToString("0.00")</span>
                </div>
            </div>
            @if (_transactionNote.Length > 0)
            {
                <p class="bb-card-desc" role="status">@_transactionNote</p>
            }
        </DemoCard>
    </div>

    <div class="bb-card">
        <h2 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-success"></span> Async actions</h2>
        <p>
            An <code>[BlexAction]</code> may return <code>Task</code> or <code>ValueTask</code>. State changes
            made before and after an <code>await</code> update the UI as they happen, but the whole
            operation is recorded as a single action.
        </p>
        <CodeBlock Code="@AsyncAction" />

        <DemoCard Title="An async action, rendered as it goes"
                  Description="ProgressStore.Run is an async [BlexAction] that moves Percent from 0 to 100 in 20 steps, 100 ms apart. Every step re-renders this page the moment it lands - count the StateChanged notifications - yet the run is recorded once, as progress/Run, when it ends."
                  Source="@AsyncDemoSource">
            <div class="bb-toolbar">
                <button class="btn btn-primary" @onclick="RunProgress" disabled="@Progress.IsRunning">
                    @(Progress.IsRunning ? "Running…" : "Run(steps: 20, delayMilliseconds: 100)")
                </button>
                <progress max="100" value="@Progress.Percent" aria-label="Run progress"></progress>
            </div>
            <div class="bb-stats">
                <div class="bb-stat">
                    <span class="bb-stat-label">progress/Percent</span>
                    <span class="bb-stat-value">@Progress.Percent%</span>
                </div>
                <div class="bb-stat">
                    <span class="bb-stat-label">StateChanged this run</span>
                    <span class="bb-stat-value demo-accent">@_progressNotifications</span>
                </div>
                <div class="bb-stat">
                    <span class="bb-stat-label">Actions recorded this run</span>
                    <span class="bb-stat-value demo-accent">@_progressActions</span>
                </div>
            </div>
            <p class="bb-card-desc">
                The undo history gains one step for the whole run, and the Redux DevTools one entry carrying its
                payload, <code>{ "steps": 20, "delayMilliseconds": 100 }</code>. The <a href="subscriptions#throttle">render
                throttling demo</a> runs the same action faster, to show <code>RenderInterval</code> capping the
                renders it causes.
            </p>
        </DemoCard>

        <p>
            If what you are writing is a side-effect with a loading and error lifecycle, reach for
            <a href="effects"><code>[BlexEffect]</code></a> instead - it generates all of that for you.
        </p>

        <Callout Kind="Callout.CalloutKind.Note" Title="Each async action records what its own flow changed">
            <p>
                An async action is recorded when it ends, with what its own body changed - across its awaits, and
                nothing else. A click handled while it awaits, another action of the same store, a second run of
                it started meanwhile: each is recorded as an action of its own (an assignment as its own
                <code>Set X</code>, offered to the filters like any other), in the order they finish. Only an
                action dispatched from <em>inside</em> its body is part of it. Rollback is synchronous only:
                <code>RollbackOnError</code> leaves an async action alone and <code>Transaction</code> takes a
                synchronous body, since an async action has already shown the UI what it changed before its first
                <code>await</code>.
            </p>
        </Callout>

        <Callout Kind="Callout.CalloutKind.Warning" Title="Never async void">
            <p>
                An <code>async void</code> action cannot be awaited or recorded correctly, so it is a
                hard error (<code>BLEX010</code>). Return <code>Task</code> or <code>ValueTask</code>.
                Similarly, an action that returns a <em>value</em> gets warning <code>BLEX011</code>,
                because the generated wrapper discards it - put the result in a <code>[BlexState]</code>
                field instead.
            </p>
        </Callout>
    </div>

    <div class="bb-card">
        <h2 class="bb-card-title"><span class="bb-dot bb-dot-warning"></span> What a dispatch actually does</h2>
        <ol class="bb-detail-list">
            <li>Runs every middleware's <code>BeforeAction</code>. If one calls <code>Cancel()</code>, the action stops here - nothing mutates.</li>
            <li>Opens the action for the current flow, then runs your method body. Actions dispatched from inside it are part of it; an action started elsewhere meanwhile is its own.</li>
            <li>On completion, invalidates computed values and raises <code>StateChanged</code> once.</li>
            <li>Records the action: middleware <code>OnAction</code> hooks run, then DevTools receives it, then subscribers.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>
            The global state snapshot attached to an action is captured <em>lazily</em>, on first access.
            Observers that never read it - persistence, for instance - never pay to serialize the whole
            tree. When nothing at all is observing, the recording step is skipped outright.
        </p>
    </div>

</DocPage>

